%!testlib
% What a page-device request may state, run through
% `xpost -q --no-sandbox -d null` by run-ps-test.sh.
%
% setpagedevice merges each request into the page-device dictionary the
% last call left, and currentpagedevice reports it. What merges is the
% page-device parameters and nothing else: a key that is not one of them
% is accepted -- the request is not refused over it -- and dropped, so it
% reaches neither the report nor the device.
%
% Dropping is what keeps a request inside the parameters. A device
% dictionary holds the procedures the machinery paints through under
% ordinary names in the same dictionary the parameters live in, so a key
% carried across from a request could name one of those and replace it,
% and every later mark would go through the program's procedure instead.
% It also settles the accumulation: a program cannot name a fresh key on
% every call, so the dictionary copied whole on each call cannot grow
% past the parameters there are.

% A rich request naming every standard parameter at once is far below the
% bound and is accepted.
{ << /PageSize [612 792] /HWResolution [72 72] /ImagingBBox null
     /Orientation 0 /NumCopies 1 /MediaColor (white) /MediaType (plain)
     /Duplex false /Tumble false /Collate false /BeginPage {} /EndPage {}
     /Install {} /Policies << /PageSize 3 >> >> setpagedevice }
    (a request naming the standard parameters is accepted) works

% The same keys set again and again do not accumulate: each put replaces a
% key already there, so the count does not move. Six hundred such requests
% -- more than the bound -- prove it by not reaching the bound; were the
% keys accumulating, this many would cross it and raise.
{ 1 1 600 { pop << /PageSize [1 1] /NumCopies 1 >> setpagedevice } for }
    (repeated identical requests do not accumulate to the bound) works

% A key that is no page-device parameter is accepted and dropped: the
% request goes through, and the key reaches neither the report nor the
% accumulated dictionary. So naming a fresh one on every call cannot
% grow that dictionary at all.
currentpagedevice length /pd.n exch def
{ << /PageSize [612 792] /NoSuchParameter 42 >> setpagedevice }
    (a request naming something that is not a parameter is accepted) works
currentpagedevice /NoSuchParameter known not
    (and the key it named is not reported back) assert
{ 1 1 200 { /i exch def
    << /PageSize [612 792] i 20 string cvs cvn i >> setpagedevice } for }
    (naming a fresh key on every call raises nothing) works
currentpagedevice length pd.n le
    (and leaves the accumulated dictionary no larger than it found it) assert

% What accumulates is bounded, and Policies is what accumulates.
%
% A policy may be stated for a parameter this interpreter does not
% provide, and it is kept (PLRM 6.1.1 makes the entries cumulative and
% does not hold the keys to the parameters there are). The accumulated
% dictionary is copied whole on every call, so a program naming a fresh
% policy per call would cost the square of its calls and grow without
% ceiling. A request that carries the count past the bound is refused
% rather than served.
{ 1 1 200 { /i exch def
    << /Policies << i 20 string cvs cvn 1 >> >> setpagedevice } for }
    (policies well inside the bound accumulate) works
currentpagedevice /Policies get length 200 ge
    (and every one of them is kept) assert
{ 1 1 600 { /i exch def
    << /Policies << i 1000 add 20 string cvs cvn 1 >> >> setpagedevice } for }
    /limitcheck (naming a fresh policy on every call is refused) raises

% A device's own painting procedures share the dictionary the parameters
% live in, so the drop is what keeps a request from reaching one. Every
% device carries Destroy and Emit; a request naming either is accepted
% and neither reaches the device.
{ << /Destroy { } /Emit { } >> setpagedevice }
    (a request naming a device's own procedures is accepted) works
currentpagedevice /Destroy known not
    (and Destroy is not taken from it) assert
currentpagedevice /Emit known not
    (and neither is Emit) assert

% PLRM 6.1: what currentpagedevice returns is read-only. It is built
% fresh on each call, so a write to it could never be seen by the next
% one; refusing the write says so, where accepting it would leave a
% program believing it had set a parameter it had not.
currentpagedevice wcheck not
    (what currentpagedevice returns is read-only) assert
{ currentpagedevice /PageSize [1 1] put } /invalidaccess
    (and a write to it is refused) raises
currentpagedevice /PageSize get 0 get 1 ne
    (so the refused write did not take) assert

verdict
